package ${packagePath};

import io.koraframework.common.annotation.KoraSubmodule;

/**
 * Example @KoraSubmodule — how a Gradle subproject exports its Kora components.
 *
 * <p>The processor generates {@code ${Module}ModuleSubmoduleImpl} next to this interface,
 * containing a provider for every @Component class and every @Module provider method compiled in
 * THIS Gradle subproject, carrying over their @Tag, @Root and @DefaultComponent annotations.
 * The assembly project's @KoraApp then extends this interface and the generated companion is
 * wired in automatically.
 *
 * <p>An empty body is normal — the interface is only the handle; the generated companion carries
 * the contents.
 *
 * <p>Required build wiring in THIS subproject, or nothing is generated
 * ("Kora submodule was not generated yet: expected type: ...SubmoduleImpl"):
 * <pre>
 *   // Java
 *   annotationProcessor "io.koraframework:annotation-processors"
 *
 *   // Kotlin (ksp is not covered by the BOM platform, so it needs an explicit version)
 *   ksp("io.koraframework:symbol-processors:2.0.0.RC2")
 * </pre>
 *
 * <p>Use {@code api} (not {@code implementation}) for the Kora modules this interface extends —
 * the assembly project has to see those types to extend this interface at all.
 *
 * <p>Usage from the assembly project:
 * <pre>
 *   &#64;KoraApp
 *   public interface Application extends ${Module}Module, HoconConfigModule, LogbackModule {
 *       static void main(String[] args) {
 *           KoraApplication.run(ApplicationGraph::graph);
 *       }
 *   }
 * </pre>
 *
 * <p>See: references/kora-submodule-reference.md
 * <p>See: references/module-auto-discovery-reference.md
 */
@KoraSubmodule
public interface ${Module}Module {
    // Optional: factory methods shared by everything in this subproject.
    // @Component classes and @Module interfaces elsewhere in this subproject are exported
    // automatically — they do not need to be mentioned here.
}
